Current dispersive readout schemes couple a superconducting qubit to a linear resonator. Baseline achieves SNR ~ 3–5 in 1 µs with moderate QND fidelity (~95%). The bottleneck is the Purcell decay and the amplifier noise temperature (typically > 2K).
**The 60-point ceiling of the old route has exhausted all degrees of freedom in coupling strength and integration time—further increasing the coupling induces unwanted qubit transitions (saturation), and further extending the integration time allows decoherence to dominate. Its limit is not technological, but physical: the quantum efficiency of the chain is capped by the added noise of the first-stage amplifier and the inevitable leakage of the resonator into the feedline.**

